arly 1970s, late 1970s, and mid-1980s. Each peak represents a temporary economic growth phase before a subsequent downturn.\n\n2. \"Gross Saving as a Percent of GDP\" (1960-1988): This plot would likely display a general declining trend with some fluctuations. The saving rate would show periodic peaks around the early 1960s, mid-1970s, and early 1980s, capturing the nuanced variations in national saving behavior during these decades.\n\n**For Exhibit 2:**\n\"U.S. GROSS SAVING RATIO, 1898-1990\": This extensive time series would capture the long-term evolution of the US saving rate, with notable peaks before the Great Depression, during World War II, and potentially in the late 1940s or early 1950s.\n\nI recognize the complexity of precisely determining local maxima without direct chart access. My analysis relies on interpreting textual descriptions and understanding historical economic patterns, which introduces inherent uncertainty in countin the exact number of peaks.\n","signature":""},{"type":"toolRequest","id":"call_3534eb4576d24144a6cf081f","toolCall":{"status":"success","value":{"name":"shell","arguments":{"command":"grep -n \"GDP\\|saving\\|GNP\" /app/resources/treasury_bulletin_1990_09.json | head -50"}}},"_meta":{"goose_extension":"developer"}}],"metadata":{"userVisible":true,"agentVisible":true}}}
